      "created_at": "2019-05-27T09:19:46Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-05-27T09:19:46Z",
      "body": "> > Try that on desktop and Fennec and you'll see that it's not normal behaviour.\r\n> \r\n> There is no \"normal\" behaviour, you can argue for both behaviours. It's a decision made by the developers to use either the one or the other mode. And yes, there may be different opinions. But in my opinion the current behaviour of Fenix makes a lot of sense and I don't want the current behaviour changed.\r\n\r\nI disagree. Normal is legacy behaviour. To have browser traditionally act one way and then suddenly change that and is abnormal behaviour. It's confusing and bewildering.\r\n\r\n> Fenix has another UX than Firefox for Desktop or Fennec. The mask icon on the Fenix home screen acts as a kind of \"permanent browsing mode toggle\", Fenix remembers the choice. If I press the mask icon it's my explicit decision to use the private mode, also the next time when I start Fenix. Just try it without using an external app: Start Fenix, switch to private mode, exit Fenix via Android recent apps, start Fenix again. As you can see you're still in private mode. That's intended behaviour. So there is no reason why Fenix should behave differently when clicking a link in an external app.\r\n> \r\n> And to be honest, being accidentally in the private mode is far less a problem than the other way around.\r\n\r\nI can totally see the benefits for such an approach, they don't fit how I use a browser's private mode, but then I've had years to learn my current behaviour model of wanting to open something privately and expecting my browser to treat everything else as normal browsing behaviour. I'm really not arguing against the change in browser behaviour, I just hope that users can be notified that there has been a change in behaviour and that desktop and mobile act the same. I would also, and this is the most important part, expect the UI to suggest that my actions entail a permanent change in state and thus I would like to advocate for switching out the button for a toggle.\r\n\r\n\r\n> Funnily in #2868 you argued for privacy by default a few minutes ago but here you don't want the more private approach.\r\n\r\nI advocated for an option, but you're ultimately right. That said, I continue to want users to be informed and in control. Also, once again I'd like to advocate for switching from a button to a toggle seeing that private browsing is a sticky mode now, I feel that the change should definitely be represented with a switch/toggle and not a button. That should allay some of the confusion that users are likely to feel.\r\n",
      "author_association": "NONE",
